Design a priority-based arbiter that manages four input signals with two priority levels. The module first checks for active input signals in the highest priority group (priority 0) and, if available, uses a round-robin method to select one of these inputs to output. If no signals from priority 0 are active, it checks the lower priority group (priority 1) and similarly selects from active inputs using round-robin. If no inputs are active, the output is set to LOW. This arbiter effectively ensures fair access to shared resources based on defined priorities, making it suitable for applications where signal contention occurs.
module top_module(
  input [n-1:0] in,
  output reg out
);

code:
module top_module(
  input [n-1:0] in,
  output reg out
);parameter n = 4; // number of input signals
parameter p = 2; // number of priority levels

// Define the priority levels
// Each priority level is associated with a set of input signals
parameter [n-1:0] priority_0 = 4'b1100; // input signals with priority 0
parameter [n-1:0] priority_1 = 4'b0011; // input signals with priority 1

// Define the priority rules
// If two or more input signals have the same priority, use round-robin
reg [1:0] round_robin = 2'b00;

always @(*) begin
  if (priority_0 & in) begin
    case (round_robin)
      2'b00: out = in[0];
      2'b01: out = in[1];
      2'b10: out = in[2];
      2'b11: out = in[3];
    endcase
    round_robin = round_robin + 1;
  end else if (priority_1 & in) begin
    case (round_robin)
      2'b00: out = in[0];
      2'b01: out = in[1];
      2'b10: out = in[2];
      2'b11: out = in[3];
    endcase
    round_robin = round_robin + 1;
  end else begin
    out = 1'b0;
  end
end

endmodule

